17592186344984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087749 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087749 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087749 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087749 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087749 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087932 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fd6a29fe-5312-5d49-b1ae-79d4178dc9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088602 #edb/ref 1061 19995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b716871b-bb81-5bf2-8223-80c9510b7921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088603 #edb/ref 1061 19996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15549c3e-d1ae-52a9-b57f-520764e652b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088604 #edb/ref 1061 19997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c11e935e-6e42-5053-8e60-e60d238b8a80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088605 #edb/ref 1061 19998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b01168ef-ba36-595a-90db-36c9c6fc6b90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088606 #edb/ref 1061 19999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f470406-9e89-58e2-bd83-097c8b921250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088637 #edb/ref 1061 23253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e84e1380-e6a4-5d10-9efe-3491fa692281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088928 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nThis function $f$ takes a real number $x$ as input, and it outputs a real number. We can describe this fact using the following notation:\n\n$$\nf: x \\longrightarrow 2x+1\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can define a function $\\mathbf T,$ also called a **transformation** or a **map**, that takes a 2D-vector (or 3D-vector) as input and outputs a new 2D-vector (or, respectively, 3D-vector). \n\nFor example, let's consider the following transformation: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that for any 2D-vector $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ the corresponding image $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this transformation just like we would with any other function. For example, applying the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ to the input vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that the transformation $\\mathbf T$ *maps* the input vector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ to the output vector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088928 #edb/ref 1065 3525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088928 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58dc3c80-54e0-549f-afc1-e5a3b8cbea52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088928 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088940 #edb/ref 1064 "A transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is called a **linear transformation** (or **linear map**) if the following two properties are satisfied:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $k$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$\n\nIf we let $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then the conditions above can be written as follows:\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\n\nWe can determine whether the transformation $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ is linear by checking if these two conditions are true.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)+(ky) \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so this transformation is linear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088940 #edb/ref 1065 3935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088940 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5951013b-06e0-5923-bbd4-d8440d67679e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088940 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282528 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282528 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282528 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "624f1134-c016-5bc0-8b8d-60ab4b3ca722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283118 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283118 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283118 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a417abba-876d-599b-9802-9bdfb1ffd201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283238 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283238 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283238 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2976f83-d90a-5462-9538-2b4fd5f9d325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283329 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd03a3d9-4387-5a6d-83af-e38b3d0f76f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283581 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283581 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283581 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d93c61fd-4ae2-5980-8870-7696c25651e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284246 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b37cc56a-bb1a-5c82-a7b5-3e63dd5b826f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "392b472a-8eb5-510e-870b-90564b90ad03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284660 #edb/ref 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
